  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 PM Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Dry and increasingly warm weather expected \nthroughout the week, followed by rising rain chances by the weekend. \n\nDeep low pressure is holding over and just off New England today. \nAround this low, we remain in a cyclonic but dry flow pattern with \nlow PW, particularly across the S and W CWA where PW is sub-1\" and \nwhere a low-mid level downslope component is further drying out the \ncolumn. Scattered to occasionally broken clouds will stream over NE \nsections through tonight, as minor perturbations rotate through the \nbase of the positive-leaning mid level trough sitting over the Mid \nAtlantic, but otherwise fair skies are expected through tonight. \n\nDeeply dry air and below normal PW will stay in place through much \nof this week. As the New England low weakens and lifts NE into the \nCanadian Maritimes, the trough axis will become increasingly weak \nand baggy and succumb to building heights aloft over the Carolinas, \nMid South, and Southeast. This will resulting in rising low-level \nthicknesses each day through Sat, with highs in the 80s Tue through \nSat, and lower 90s common on Thu/Fri. \n\nA pattern change is expected by the weekend, as the deep closed low \nnow over central CA/NV drifts through the Southwest states, opens up \nwhile shifting into the Midwest/Ohio Valley through mid week, then \njoins up with polar stream energy to dig broad troughing over the \neastern Great Lakes and Northeast over the weekend. This will tamp \ndown our mid level ridge and shunt it eastward, allowing for an \nincreasing and deepening moisture flux from FL and the Gulf. Models \ndiffer a bit on how much moisture return will occur Fri, but they \nagree on a surge of high PW (near 200% of normal) by Sat, lasting \nthrough Sun, with forecast soundings showing an LCL-0C depth over 4 \nkm, favoring warm rain processes and boosting the heavy rainfall \npotential. This possible threat will be closely monitored in the \ncoming days.",
